/**
 @page benchmarks_swift2_hydro_kernel_throughput Kernel throughput benchmark
 
 This is a short benchmark which assesses the kernel throughputs of the Swift 2
 project. In our own research, we use this benchmark primarily to study the 
 impact of our proposed C++ language modifications. Obviously,  the benchmark
 can also be used to assess a machinery.
 
 ## Design
 
 The benchmark design is very simple. We first create a simple SPH test through 
 the full-blown Swift 2 software stack. Before we compile, we however replace
 the main file with our bespoke benchmark main, which is really just a sequence
 of loops.
 
 
 The benchmark is guided by few key parameters
 
 - Number of particles. The benchmark driver iterates over those particles 
   internally. You can alter the maximum particles count however (invoke the 
   Python script with --help for details).
 - Each test is repeated sample times. The default is 16, but you can alter it 
   through the Python script.
 - The number of particles is split up into work items, i.e. chunks of 
   continuous particles. For a real setup, we can think of these work items
   as the number of particles associated typically with a vertex while we 
   kick them, or we can read them as the average number of particles per 
   cell which have to be compared to each other. Lacking a better name, we 
   use the generic term work item here and make its size configurable. 
 - The benchmark runs over a sequence of work items and invokes the kernels
   over all particles per work item.  
   If you have a linear kernel (e.g. the drift or kick), the 
   script runs through all particles per work item. If you have a quadratic
   kernel (force and density) the benchmark runs over all particles of a 
   work item checked against all other particles of this work item. That
   is, the kernel benchmark works with quadratic complexity over the 
   work item size, but linear complexity overall. We do not write these loops
   here ourselves, but we directly invoke the Swift 2 loop routines, i.e. 
   everything we measure is 1:1 from the Swift 2 code base.
 - We always use the masked variant of the kernel, i.e. the one which computes
   all particle updates and then applies these updates subject to a final 
   distance check. This is likely not the most efficient way for many real 
   setups, but it yields kind of deterministic workload per work item.
 - We can control on the Python level if we want to invoke the kernel once
   or multiple times. If you pick ```--work-item-repeats 1''', the kernel
   behaviour very much resembles a loop-based SPH realisation: All particles
   are kicked, then all are drifted, ... Notably, each kernel might have to 
   load all data through the bus. If you use a higher value, such as 
   ```--work-item-repeats 32''', you apply the kernel multiple times in a row.
   This is similar to a task-based approach, where subsequent phases on a small
   chunk of particles (i.e. a work item) immediately follow each other. The 
   particels therefore likely remain in a closeby cache.
 - The benchmark also supports a flag --cache-prefetch. If set, the test
   driver tries to bring the particles of a work item into the local cache
   before we start a measurement.

 
 ## Build a demo

 The build can all be triggered through the command line using the Python 
 interface.
  
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 export PYTHONPATH=../../../../python
 python3 kernel-throughput.py --dimensions 2 
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 
 This is the baseline version using some default arguments. You might want 
 to tailor the settings. The build yields an executable which you should
 be able to run without further user interference.
 
 
 ## Run suite of experiments
 
 This is a normal test case which we run to produce the plots for one of our 
 papers:

 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 export MAX_PARTICLES=100000
 export MACHINE=mymachine
 
 mkdir -p $MACHINE-single-touch
 python3 kernel-throughput.py --dimensions 2 --samples 16 --work-item-size 64 --work-item-repeats 1 --max-particle-number $MAX_PARTICLES --enable-kernel drift force
 # for THREADS in 01 02 04 08 16 32
 for THREADS in 01 02 03 06 12 24
 do
   echo "Running on $THREADS thread(s) at $(date)"
   OMP_NUM_THREADS=$THREADS numactl --membind=0 --cpunodebind=0 ./kernel-benchmarks > $MACHINE-single-touch/original-$THREADS-threads.log
 done
 
 mkdir -p $MACHINE-multitouch
 python3 kernel-throughput.py --dimensions 2 --samples 8 --work-item-size 64 --work-item-repeats 32 --max-particle-number $MAX_PARTICLES --cache-prefetch --enable-kernel drift force
 # for THREADS in 01 02 04 08 16 32
 for THREADS in 01 02 03 06 12 24
 do
   echo "Running on $THREADS thread(s) at $(date)"
   OMP_NUM_THREADS=$THREADS numactl --membind=0 --cpunodebind=0 ./kernel-benchmarks > $MACHINE-multitouch/original-$THREADS-threads.log
 done
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 
 Now you have to switch compilers to enable the compression and rerun the same 
 sequence. The output file however should not start with original- anymore but
 instead with compressed-, i.e. files should be called compressed-8-threads.log
 for example.
 

 ## Postprocessing
 
 There's a postprocessing script in the directory, which expects the naming 
 convention as of above. It operates on a per-directory basis:
 
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 python3 ./postprocess.py mymachine-single-touch
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  
  
 ## Assessing individual kernels

 It is possible to assess individual kernels. First, we can narrow down the 
 measurement to individual particles counts:
 
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 python3 kernel-throughput.py --dimensions 2 --cache-prefetch --min-particle-number 65536 --max-particle-number 65536
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 
 Further to that, we can pick only certain kernels. If you type in --help, 
 you see all kernels that we offer as part of the benchmark. By default,
 we test all of them. If you use the --enable-kernel argument, all kernels
 are, by default, switched off and you pick one or multiple kernels from the
 list.

 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 python3 kernel-throughput.py --dimensions 2 --enable-kernel drift
 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
 
 */
